The function first creates a temporary file with the same name as the MAT file but with a ".mat" extension. It then reads the MAT file into this temporary file using the `ReadMATFile` function from the MATLAB Compiler Runtime (MCR). Next, it opens the temporary file and creates a clone of the image to be read. This clone is used for decompressing the data in the MAT file.

The main loop of the function reads scanlines from the MAT file into a buffer and imports them into the image using the `ImportQuantumPixels` function. The buffer size (ldblk) depends on the depth of the pixels in the MAT file, which can be 8-bit integer, unsigned integer, or single-precision floating-point.

After all scanlines have been read and imported, the complex parts of the numbers are read and inserted into the image if necessary. The function also rotates the image by 90 degrees before returning it. If there are multiple frames in the MAT file, the function loops through them and returns a list of images.

Upon completion, the temporary file is deleted, and any empty images are removed from the list. If a garbage file was created during the compression process, it is also cleaned up. The function returns the final image or list of images as output.

Overall, this function demonstrates how to read MAT files using MCR and convert them into an image format in C. It's important to note that this code requires the MCR library to be installed on the system.

